    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5e59d046-9084-5e63-8b57-52d817af0c87",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{4x+3}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=4x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={5}^{u}\\ln(5),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={5}^{u}\\ln(5)\\cdot (4) \\\\ & =(4)\\cdot {5}^{u}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =4\\cdot {5}^{4x+3}\\ln(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=-1} & =(4)\\cdot {5}^{4(-1)+3}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =4\\cdot {5}^{-1}\\ln(5) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=-1$ is $\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={5}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{5})&=(\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5})\\left(x-(-1)\\right) \\\\ y&=\\frac{4 x \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
