      ":question/id": "608ed9ee-ed1f-594e-adfb-3735fbb60f01",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{-6x-1}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=-6x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ & =(-6)\\cdot {4}^{u}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-6\\cdot {4}^{-6x-1}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(-6)\\cdot {4}^{-6(0)-1}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =-6\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{2}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={4}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{4})&=(- \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{2})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=- 3 x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
