    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aacd1525-9e51-583b-8dc4-6cd481ed6930",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={2}^{-7x-2}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=-7x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={2}^{u}\\ln(2),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={2}^{u}\\ln(2)\\cdot (-7) \\\\ & =(-7)\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =-7\\cdot {2}^{-7x-2}\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(-7)\\cdot {2}^{-7(0)-2}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =-7\\cdot {2}^{-2}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={2}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{4})&=(- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{7 x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
