      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={3}^{9x-2}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={3}^{u},\\,u=9x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={3}^{u}\\ln(3),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={3}^{u}\\ln(3)\\cdot (9) \\\\ & =(9)\\cdot {3}^{u}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =9\\cdot {3}^{9x-2}\\ln(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=0} & =(9)\\cdot {3}^{9(0)-2}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =9\\cdot {3}^{-2}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={3}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{9})&=(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)})\\left(x-(0)\\right) \\\\ y&=x \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} + \\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
